1 true] [17592186325359 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $D$, $E$, $F$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=⟨1,\\,3,\\,2⟩,\\,e=⟨t,\\,2,\\,4⟩,\\,f=⟨3,\\,t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△DEF$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠EDF=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045824/assets/c9cfb3e84e6f76d243125f2a09246b2a1300a0134e71359bb2b528082f064f93.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,2,\\,4⟩-⟨1,\\,3,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,t,\\,3⟩-⟨1,\\,3,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,t-3,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=2$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =2 \\\\ (t-1)\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (t-3)+2\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ 2t-2-t+3+2 & =2 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨-1-1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨2,-1-3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}}{|\\overset{\\to }{DE}||\\overset{\\to }{DF}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{21}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{21}})≈{81.64}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534991 true] [17592186325360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325361 13194139534991 true] [17592186325360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325362 13194139534991 true] [17592186325360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325363 13194139534991 true] [17592186325360 1